README.md:41:The most important feature of stegseek is wordlist cracking:
README.md:43:stegseek [stegofile.jpg] [wordlist.txt]
README.md:46:This mode will simply try all passwords in the provided wordlist against the provided stegofile.
README.md:49:Stegseek can also be used to detect and extract any **unencrypted** (meta) data from a steghide image. This exploits the fact that the random number generator used in steghide only has 2^32 possible seeds, which can be bruteforced in a matter of minutes.  
README.md:52:stegseek --seed [stegofile.jpg]
README.md:64:![](./.demo/seed.gif "Sped up by a factor of 3 for your viewing pleasure.")
README.md:68:Use `stegseek --help` to get the full list of available options:
README.md:72:stegseek [stegofile.jpg] [wordlist.txt]
README.md:75: --crack                 Crack a stego file using a wordlist. This is the default mode.
README.md:76: --seed                  Crack a stego file by attempting all embedding patterns.
README.md:81: --crack [stegofile.jpg] [wordlist.txt] [output.txt]
README.md:82: --seed  [stegofile.jpg] [output.txt]
README.md:86: -wl, --wordlist         select the wordlist file
README.md:88: -t, --threads           set the number of threads. Defaults to the number of cores.
README.md:92: -s, --skipdefault       don't add guesses to the wordlist (empty password, filename, ...)
README.md:93: -n, --nocolor           disable colors in output
README.md:96: -a, --accessible        simplify the output to be more screen reader friendly
README.md:98:Use "stegseek --help -v" to include steghide's help.
README.md:107:* `stegseek --extract <stegofile> [<output>]`
README.md:113:docker run --rm -it -v "$(pwd):/steg" rickdejager/stegseek [stegofile.jpg] [wordlist.txt]
README.md:116:This does require that the wordlist and stegofile are located in current working directory, as that folder is mounted to `/steg` inside of the container.
README.md:129:[i] Found passphrase: "       1"          
README.md:143:To test the performance of of other tools, I created several stego files with different passwords, taken from `rockyou.txt`. I ran each of the tools with their default settings, except Stegbrute where I increased threading for a fair comparison.
README.md:159:To compare the speed of each tool, let's look at the last row of the table (otherwise Stegseek finishes before all threads have started).  
README.md:172:* `--crack` and `--seed` now throw proper exit codes for easier scripting.
README.md:179:* Uses way less memory (memory usage is no longer tied to Wordlist length)
README.md:181:* Fixed high false positive rate on `--seed`  
README.md:186:* Added seed cracking to allow for passwordless data extraction
README.md:187:* Overhauled parser to allow for positional arguments
README.md:196:* Made threading lock-free s.t. multiple threads are used more efficiently
README.md:204:* Give up on a passphrase once a single bit of the magic fails to decode
README.md:213:* Workpool based threading
